# One comparison, total. As an ordinary program.
#
# This is `types.compare`, which `goal.holds`, `criterion._holds` and every schema check share — so `>=`
# cannot come to mean different things in a `type` block and in a goal. That sharing is the reason this
# file exists at all: the moment a predicate written in the surface needs to compare two values, either
# it calls this or it grows a second comparator, and a second comparator is the drift this codebase keeps
# finding.
#
# **Totality is the whole difficulty.** `LT` is Python's `<`, so comparing a string to a number raises,
# and a schema is checked against whatever the world happens to hold — the world being under no
# obligation to cooperate. Python catches the `TypeError`; a program here cannot catch anything, so it
# has to **ask first**, and asking is what `VKIND` was added for.
#
# ⚠ It deliberately reproduces one of Python's rules rather than improving on it: a truth compares with a
# number, because `True < 2` is True there and `types.compare` is the comparator this has to agree with.
# Being stricter here would be defensible in isolation and wrong in context — there is one comparison,
# and two spellings of it that disagree about a single pair is exactly the failure the shared comparator
# exists to prevent.
#
# NOT live for Python callers, and that is a decision rather than an omission: `types.compare` sits under
# every schema check, which is the hottest path in the system, and swapping an interpreted call in there
# would be paid on every `is_a` in every search. The two are checked against each other instead, and this
# one is what the surface calls.

# Can these two values be ORDERED at all? Equality never needs this — `==` never raises in Python — so
# only `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=` and `between` ask.
#
# Two texts order, and two numbers order. `nothing`, `unknown` and anything else do not order even
# against themselves: `None < None` raises exactly as `None < 1` does, and answering `false` for it is
# the totality this is here to provide.
#
# ⚠ That is stricter than Python in exactly one place, and it is worth stating rather than discovering:
# Python orders two tuples, and a value of no named category answers `false` here. Measured over every
# operator against every pair drawn from numbers, truths, texts, `nothing`, `UNKNOWN` and a tuple — the
# only two disagreements in about a thousand are `() <= ()` and `() >= ()`. Nothing authors a
# tuple-valued constraint: the CNL cannot write one and no schema declares one. The alternative is
# ordering arbitrary objects on the strength of Python's rules for them, which is a much larger promise
# for a much smaller return.
fn value_orderable(a, b) -> thing:
    VKIND R(ka) F(a)
    VKIND R(kb) F(b)
    EQ R(a_text) R(ka) "text"
    EQ R(b_text) R(kb) "text"
    JMPNOT R(a_text) .numeric
    COPY R(result) R(b_text)
    JMP .end
.numeric:
    INVOKE R(a_num) value_numeric k=R(ka)
    JMPNOT R(a_num) .no
    INVOKE R(b_num) value_numeric k=R(kb)
    COPY R(result) R(b_num)
    JMP .end
.no:
    CONST R(result) false
.end:
    CONST R(done) true


# Is this value category one that orders as a number? A truth is one, because Python's `bool` is an
# `int` and the comparator this agrees with inherits that.
fn value_numeric(k) -> thing:
    EQ R(is_num) F(k) "number"
    JMPIF R(is_num) .yes
    EQ R(is_truth) F(k) "truth"
    JMPIF R(is_truth) .yes
    CONST R(result) false
    JMP .end
.yes:
    CONST R(result) true
.end:
    CONST R(done) true


# `got op want`, with `hi` as the upper bound of `between`. Answers false — never raises — for values
# that cannot be compared, and for an operator nothing declared: refuse, never assume.
fn compare(op, got, want, hi) -> thing:
    EQ R(is_eq) F(op) "=="
    JMPNOT R(is_eq) .try_ne
    EQ R(result) F(got) F(want)
    JMP .end
.try_ne:
    EQ R(is_ne) F(op) "!="
    JMPNOT R(is_ne) .try_between
    EQ R(same) F(got) F(want)
    NOT R(result) R(same)
    JMP .end

    # `want <= got <= hi`, and both halves need their own guard: the bound and the value are two
    # different pairs, and a world holding a string where a range was declared fails the first.
.try_between:
    EQ R(is_between) F(op) "between"
    JMPNOT R(is_between) .ordering
    INVOKE R(ok_lo) value_orderable a=F(want) b=F(got)
    JMPNOT R(ok_lo) .no
    INVOKE R(ok_hi) value_orderable a=F(got) b=F(hi)
    JMPNOT R(ok_hi) .no
    LT R(below) F(got) F(want)
    JMPIF R(below) .no
    LT R(above) F(hi) F(got)
    JMPIF R(above) .no
    CONST R(result) true
    JMP .end

.ordering:
    INVOKE R(ok) value_orderable a=F(got) b=F(want)
    JMPNOT R(ok) .no
    EQ R(is_lt) F(op) "<"
    JMPNOT R(is_lt) .try_le
    LT R(result) F(got) F(want)
    JMP .end
.try_le:
    EQ R(is_le) F(op) "<="
    JMPNOT R(is_le) .try_gt
    LT R(greater) F(want) F(got)
    NOT R(result) R(greater)
    JMP .end
.try_gt:
    EQ R(is_gt) F(op) ">"
    JMPNOT R(is_gt) .try_ge
    LT R(result) F(want) F(got)
    JMP .end
.try_ge:
    EQ R(is_ge) F(op) ">="
    JMPNOT R(is_ge) .no
    LT R(less) F(got) F(want)
    NOT R(result) R(less)
    JMP .end

.no:
    CONST R(result) false
.end:
    CONST R(done) true
